---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Credits from '../components/Credits.astro';
import { site } from '../site';
---

<Base title="About">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">About the series</p>
      <h1 class="display-heading">The case, and why it matters</h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section paper">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <h2>The case</h2>
      <p>
        In 1959, a jury in Bombay (now Mumbai) acquitted Naval Commander Kawas Nanavati in the killing of
        his wife’s lover. The case was a media spectacle in India and in the international press: an
        upstanding naval officer engaging in vigilante justice, and a tabloid, <em>Blitz</em>, stirring up
        sympathy for the defendant.
      </p>
      <p>
        The jury split 8–1. Higher court judges called the verdict “perverse” and inconsistent with the
        evidence, and the case went to the Supreme Court of India, which sided with them. Nanavati went to
        prison.
      </p>
      <p>
        The case came to be known, not quite accurately, as “the last jury trial in India.” But it hastened
        the end of a system that elite lawyers and politicians saw as inefficient and ill-suited to a country
        as religiously and linguistically diverse as India. In 1973, trial by jury was abolished, without
        resistance.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it matters now</h2>
      <p>
        In the United States we often take the right to a trial by jury for granted. People grumble about
        jury duty and make satirical TV shows about the characters you meet in the courtroom. Yet the jury
        remains one of the most powerful ways ordinary people can resist a state that is punishing someone
        unfairly. When lawyers and judges are motivated by political interests and their own careers, the
        value of independent jurors becomes even more striking.
      </p>
      <p>
        India’s history, from the nationalists who prized trial by jury under British rule to its quiet
        abolition after independence, shows how our sense of the jury is shaped by the political needs of
        the time and by what we imagine justice looks like. Seeing how another multicultural democracy
        weighed the risks and promise of jury trials makes clearer what is at stake for us.
      </p>

      <h2>The host</h2>
      <p set:html={site.host.bio} />

      <h2>Credits</h2>
      <Credits />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
